4. MYOCARDIAL GEOMETRY
======================================================================

The computational domain represents the patient-specific ventricular
myocardium and associated ventricular anatomical surfaces required by the
solid-mechanics formulation.

The ventricular geometry is treated as a three-dimensional deformable
continuum.

The final computational domain must retain an explicit identifier linking
the geometry to:

- patient ID
- source imaging
- segmentation source
- reconstruction stage
- geometry version
- mesh version

The geometry identifier should remain stable throughout the simulation
and result-lineage workflow.

======================================================================
5. FIBER-ORIENTATION REPRESENTATION
======================================================================

The recovered model specification uses a rule-based / atlas-based
myocardial fiber-orientation representation.

The documented transmural fiber-angle specification is:

Endocardial fiber angle:
approximately +60 degrees

Epicardial fiber angle:
approximately -60 degrees

The fiber field is therefore represented by a prescribed transmural
orientation gradient rather than subject-specific diffusion-tensor
magnetic-resonance imaging (DT-MRI) fiber mapping.

This limitation must remain explicit in the manuscript.

The fiber orientation should be treated as a controlled model input and
must remain fixed across the primary gravity sweep unless a specific
sensitivity analysis changes the fiber-orientation assumption.

Fiber-field provenance should ultimately be linked to:

- fiber-generation method
- orientation convention
- transmural interpolation method
- geometry version
- fiber-field version

======================================================================
6. CONSTITUTIVE MODEL
======================================================================

The recovered computational specification uses an anisotropic
hyperelastic myocardial solid-mechanics formulation based on a
Holzapfel–Ogden-type constitutive representation.

The formulation represents the nonlinear mechanical behavior of
myocardial tissue using an isotropic matrix contribution together with
direction-dependent fiber-related mechanical behavior.

The myocardial material is treated using a nearly incompressible
formulation.

The exact numerical material parameters must be taken from the controlled
parameter registry and/or the final executable COMSOL model.

No material parameter should be invented or back-calculated solely to
reproduce a recovered manuscript value.

The definitive parameter record is maintained in:

05_COMSOL_Model/
COMSOL_Model_Parameter_and_Boundary_Condition_Registry.txt

======================================================================
7. MECHANICAL BOUNDARY CONDITIONS
======================================================================

The computational model applies prescribed mechanical constraints to
provide a physically controlled ventricular solid-mechanics problem.

The basal region is treated using the documented basal mechanical
constraint formulation.

Where spring/damper-type basal constraints are used, the exact numerical
coefficients must be obtained from the recovered model or parameter
record.

If those exact coefficients cannot be recovered, they must be reported
internally as:

NOT CURRENTLY RECOVERED

They must not be reconstructed solely from downstream numerical results.

The computational boundary-condition configuration must remain unchanged
across the primary gravity sweep unless explicitly designated as a
gravity-dependent parameter.

